Acute Gastrointestinal Bleeding
Acute Urgent Care
Haematemesis/Melaena, Postural hypotension, Drop in haemoglobin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-007
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of vomiting blood and black tarry stools requiring immediate clinical stabilization.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient passing foul-smelling black stools and vomiting coffee-ground material, accompanied by lightheadedness upon standing. This presentation dictates an immediate, urgent assessment to evaluate for hemorrhagic shock and coordinate rapid emergency transfer, as waiting until regular clinic hours would risk catastrophic exsanguination and death.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I threw up what looked like wet coffee grounds, and when I stood up from the toilet, I nearly blacked out.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 12-hour history of severe epigastric discomfort that rapidly evolved into acute gastrointestinal bleeding. The timeline began with generalized weakness, followed by an episode of haematemesis resembling coffee grounds. Within the last 4 hours, the patient passed two episodes of melaena, described as black, tarry, and foul-smelling. The patient notes significant lightheadedness and presyncope upon standing (postural hypotension). There is noticeable pallor and a resting tachycardia. The patient denies recent NSAID use but has a history of heavy alcohol intake.
Functional Status: Bedbound; extremely dizzy when attempting to sit or stand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to profound orthostatic hypotension.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/60 | HR: 118 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient appears markedly pale, lethargic, and diaphoretic, lying supine.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals mild epigastric tenderness but no rigidity or rebound guarding. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Cardiovascular exam shows a regular but rapid and weak pulse, with delayed capillary refill of 4 seconds indicating poor peripheral perfusion. Digital rectal exam confirms the presence of black, tarry melaena on the glove. The clinical picture aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of haematemesis/melaena, and postural hypotension, with an anticipated significant drop in haemoglobin. Expected investigation results include an actively bleeding mucosal lesion or visible vessel on upper endoscopy.
Environmental Observations: Evidence of melaena in the adjoining bathroom; path to the front door is clear for stretcher access.
Medication Reconciliation: No anticoagulants or antiplatelets found. Patient admits to taking over-the-counter ibuprofen occasionally.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Gastrointestinal Bleeding - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Dietary Melanosis (e.g., Iron or Bismuth ingestion) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of accompanying haematemesis, tachycardia, and hypotension, which confirm true haemorrhage.

Lower Gastrointestinal Bleed (e.g., Diverticular bleed) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of coffee-ground haematemesis and melaena, which localize the bleeding source to the upper GI tract.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the explicit presence of blood in the vomitus and tarry stools, alongside signs of hypovolaemic shock without a high fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The objective findings of resting tachycardia, hypotension, marked pallor, and witnessed melaena confirm a significant, active upper gastrointestinal bleed. The patient is exhibiting early signs of hypovolaemic shock. An urgent home visit was completely mandated because the patient's haemodynamic instability precluded safe travel by private vehicle and required immediate on-site medical evaluation to trigger a Priority 1 ambulance dispatch. Delaying this assessment to the next in-hours period would have inevitably resulted in severe hypoperfusion, profound anaemia, and potentially fatal cardiac arrest.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Patient placed strictly supine with legs elevated to maximize cerebral perfusion. Kept strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM). Immediate 000 call for emergency ambulance dispatch.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly avoiding all alcohol and ibuprofen from now on, you remove the chemical irritants that erode your stomach lining, allowing the tissue to heal and preventing life-threatening bleeds in the future.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Should the patient lose consciousness or begin vomiting massive amounts of bright red blood, the spouse must immediately position the patient in the recovery lateral position to protect the airway and update the 000 dispatcher.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The exact source of the bleeding requires a camera test (endoscopy) in the hospital to identify and treat.
Clinical Course: Without intravenous fluids, blood transfusions, and endoscopic intervention, the bleeding is highly likely to continue and worsen.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to stand up or walk to the bathroom, as your blood pressure is very low and you will faint. Stay completely flat on your back while we wait for the paramedics.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are keeping you flat with your legs raised to ensure the blood you do have left goes directly to your brain and your heart.
Actionable Steps: Continue taking slow, deep breaths to stay calm. Do not swallow any more blood if you feel it coming up; spit it into this bowl so we can measure the volume for the hospital team.

